 of it as a docker  container that wraps all that code and  then runs it somewhere in some arbitrary  to be determined location  here we're just transforming data and  aggregating it so like a simple toy  example  and then you can compose workflows  together uh compose workflows by  connecting tasks together and so this  looks like python but it's actually a  dsl where  this function body is compiled and into  a protobuf format that flight can reason  about and all the types  are important in that flight can analyze  them and tell you beforehand at compile  time whether your workflow is going to  work or not whether it thinks it's going  to work or not  so you can all the code you saw above  you can develop and execute locally but  then once you want to deploy and scale  it you send it over to a flight backend  so you you give it your compiled  workflow and some config  and flight is this abstraction layer  that  schedules and figures out  where when and how to run it on a  kubernetes cluster  flight isn't doesn't exist in isolation  you can use  a whole suite of integrated  tools  including spark pi torch tensorflow cube  flow  and this brings me to pandara um so when  i joined the union ai team i integrated  pandera  which was a perfect fit because  it is a basically a data frame typing  library and so when  um  typing started emerging in python as a  this gradual typing approach  i was interested in  like answering the question scratching  this itch of i i really want to type my  data frames because they're like these  complex black boxes of  just data that i don't know what is in  there so  pandara  essentially adds guardrails to your  pipeline so if your pipeline is this  suspension bridge  data frames are the people traveling  through it  it helps you  it helps them not fall off and break  your your code  so  henders started off as a pandas data  frame library but as it gained adoption  um  i saw the need for expanding it to other  data frame frameworks  and so coming in the next release is i'm  happy to announce that we'll be adding  support for das moden and koalas  and um there are so many other libraries  emerging libraries out there that um  the next piece of work to do is to  support uh ex like an easy way of  extending it to other non-pandas  compliant libraries  so with pender you can encode in some  assumptions about data frames as schemas  and so this might look like data classes  a little bit or pedantic if you're  familiar with that  and schema inheritance is followed here  in that you can inherit from other  schema models and the fields that are  defined in the inherited the parent  classes will be present in the child  classes  pandara also supports indexes and regex  column matching so if you have fields or  columns that are logically grouped  together with the same validation rules  you can make very concise schemas  you can also extend pandar's built-in  validation checks so this is  checking if  the field is greater or equal to zero  and custom checks are super easy  it's just a method in the class  it's also super easy to integrate with  flight kit  uh you just have to install this plugin  and any ad task decorated function will  at runtime  check the inputs and outputs of  data frame schemas  defined with pendant or pender  and then if something goes wrong  you get informative errors so if a  column is not present in the data frame  it complains  or if a column has an unexpected data  type  or if your assumptions in this game are  violated are violated so like here  there's the presence of negative numbers  and odd numbers and  um if you provide this lazy equals true  parameter  you see that this failure cases data  frame inside  this schema errors object  provides all of the failure cases that  caused the error so it makes it super  inspectable  what exactly went wrong  and then last but not least there's  streamlit which is a you know delight to  use it's basically  uh a way of making interactive uis via  scripting so  here's the hello world app  you run streamlet run  myapp.pi and then you get a nice  beautiful web app for you to interact  with  streamlid and flight work well together  so flight has this notion of a client  called flight remote and you can use  this remote object to get  executions workflow inputs and outputs  task definitions  anything like that  and you can then grab that data and  display it on a streamlit app  okay so this is maybe the most the the  next few sides are the most involved um  so  i'll just remind you of this high level  overview the actual pipeline at the end  doesn't quite look like this  and that's because i  set forth certain requirements when  doing this project  so the first one is that it needs to  support incremental model updates and  pre-training  the second is that  i don't want to waste request fetching  data from  the noaa api  and the last is i want to implement  online evaluation metrics which i hinted  at before  so this is it's a little more busy but  um i'll talk you through it basically  the inputs of the to the flight workflow  is this genesis date time  which is when the first  ever run of this work this model will  occur and then a target date time which  is greater or equal to the genesis date  time so this is the thing that when you  schedule this job to run if say every  hour  this is a thing that increments  you have a look back window which just  tells you okay how many time steps back  in this case at an hourly resolution  does your model have access to to make  the next uh forecast  and then how many days of pre-training  do you want when you initialize your  model at the genesis date time  so  the neat trick that's happening here  you'll notice is that here's the get  latest model workflow  and then here's the get latest model  workflow here but with the previous  timestamp  so the mind-blowing thing here is that  flight  supports recursion  and what that enables  is i can  be very efficient about how i run my  workflow so  when i first run this workflow  i'm going to initialize my model so  that's that's all fine and good but once  target daytime is greater than genesis  time  i'm going to try to get the latest model  from the previous time step  which in this in the second run will be  just this initialized model so it's you  know recursion always kind of  uh boggles my mind a little bit but when  you grasp it  this basically is it's cached so this  little this little icon here means that  the outputs  of each of these  pieces of computation are cached and so  it doesn't re-run that computation it  just grabs the latest model and then  updates it  and then from there we get our forecasts  and our metrics and then we plug that  into streamline  but where's the data coming from so this  doesn't mention anything about the data  well  we need to zoom in to initialize model  and update model so within this  workflow  we're initializing the model and metrics  but then we're also getting a  the training instances and caching that  within this workflow  and same goes for update model  and this way we're kind of creating kind  of it's not a feature store but it's  kind of like a feature or a training  data cache  where if we want to reproduce this  model and reproduce this run all we have  to do is provide the same exact  parameters and we'll be able to recover  the exact same model  um  with a caveat of if the node.gov api has  somehow been updated then it won't  exactly be the same  and then finally with the model  evaluation piece um  [Music]  we want to compute  training and  validation error  in an online fashion so this is kind of  pseudo code that does this  so basically before we update the model  at any given timestamp we feed  we do like a we make a prediction given  that model  and since the model hasn't been trained  on it yet this is validation error right  then we update the model here  and then boom right in the same  place we're gonna  feed  these 