  you execute and orchestrate computation  and scale it  um  add type with flight  and then add type safety to your data  frames or data frame like objects with  pandera  and then finally make  really  nice interactive uis with streamline  and with that i  will open it up to questions  i'm happy to answer any questions  you have here's my contact and a bunch  of links  to the you know weather forecasting app  the repo  and the three projects that i  showed you today  thank you  thank you  thank you so much  and we have some questions actually let  me read for you  alexander is asking what are the  difference between flight and cloud  composer  oh i'm not super familiar with cloud  composer so i i am aware that there are  a lot of  dag based  ml or data processing ml orchestration  tools out there  um i know somewhere we've we've done a  comparison there's i think there's a  blog or we're working on a blog i  but sorry i i'm not i'm not  knowledgeable about um enough about a  cloud composer to answer  i think so yeah  of course always you can move to the  talk to continue more discussions but  yes  um ali or ali sorry if i pronounce my  name  is asking what is the main difference  between flight and air flow  this i can say a little bit more about  so the history of flight is that it was  started at lyft  and actually the team that built flight  initially built it on top of airflow  um  and i need to like you know  get my story straight here but  like essentially they found certain  requirements that they had it with that  was not fulfilled by airflow i need to i  can get back to you um ali about that  what those specific needs were  but they determined that okay we're  gonna go to  we'll have dockerized tasks and  orchestrate it with kubernetes  and i haven't used airflow before but  i'm not sure if the back end of that is  kubernetes um you can correct me if i'm  wrong  um  but that that's the main differentiator  of flight it is is that it's um  kubernetes first and so it's basically  an abstraction layer  on top of kubernetes  that you know abstracts away a lot of  things that a lot of configuration a lot  of like scheduling things like that and  adds a few more little pieces of  functionality that kubernetes does not  offer  okay thank you  alex is pandera like pike uh i'm not  sure panderic or panda dick or pandemic  but for data frames  yeah yes exactly so  i think the two projects i think  pedentic is slightly older than pandara  um but that's that's pretty much exactly  right so pandora's  original syntax which is still supported  is more like object it's like a  dictionary of column keys and columns  um but then a contributor made a  pedantic like interface so now it looks  very much like pedantic and then  actually the next release  it's going to be  a valid  pedantic type so you can use it in your  pedantic models  yeah  okay and i will finish with this just  because they are they are um  to alex  yeah yes from alex to what extent is  flight a perfect alternative  yeah so prefect we are very much aware  of prefect um  so i  you know i need to  again educate myself more about this but  uh  i think from my understanding prefix  back end is desk  and a flight's back end is kubernetes  so in a sense  and and flight is is built to be  language agnostic so the contain if your  container contains any language's code  like c or r or julia  you could use the python sdk and you  know point to that image and give it  some inputs and it would work in your  workflow so  flight is is  um  if your team or if you are you are  familiar with kubernetes and you want to  take advantage of that  then um  flight sort of the flight is the logical  choice  um  but i don't know too much about prefect  and the advantages of prefect so  um i'd be curious to learn more though  okay thank you very much we have still  like uh three more questions  so kindly invite you to go to the box  channel  i will stop the record and thank you  again so much